WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.200
Okay, good evening everyone, can you hear me at the

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.130
back?

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.800
Alright, let's get started.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:14.750
Last week we talked about decision trees, and we saw

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.340
that a single deep tree tends to overfit.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:23.290
It has low bias, but very high variance, so if

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.640
you change the training data a little, the tree can

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:29.330
look completely different.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.280
So today the question is, how do we keep the

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.490
low bias but bring the variance down?

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.440
The idea is called bagging, which is short for bootstrap

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:43.370
aggregating.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.040
First, the bootstrap part.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:50.990
From a training set of n samples, we draw n

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.680
samples with replacement.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:57.630
Because we sample with replacement, some points appear twice or

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:01.600
three times, and some do not appear at all.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.550
In fact, the probability that a given point is never

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:10.900
picked is one minus one over n, to the power

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:11.830
n.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.780
When n is large, this goes to one over e,

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:18.850
which is about 0.368.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.800
So each bootstrap sample contains roughly 63.2 percent of the

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.490
distinct original points.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.440
Remember this number, it is a very popular quiz question.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:33.110
Second, the aggregating part.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:38.060
We train one model on each bootstrap sample, say B

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:38.990
models.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.940
For regression we average the B predictions, and for classification

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.390
we take a majority vote.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.060
Why does this help?

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:54.010
If you average B models whose errors are independent, the

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.220
variance drops by a factor of B.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:02.170
But in practice the trees are correlated, because they are

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.620
trained on very similar data.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:09.570
If the pairwise correlation is rho, the variance of the

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.920
average is rho times sigma squared, plus one minus rho

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.370
over B times sigma squared.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.320
So when B goes to infinity, the second term disappears,

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.910
but the first term, rho sigma squared, stays.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:29.860
This means adding more trees never hurts, but it stops

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:31.930
helping after a while.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.120
The real limit is the correlation between trees.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:40.690
And this is exactly the motivation for random forests.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:45.640
A random forest is bagging with decision trees, plus one

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:46.950
extra trick.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.900
At every split, instead of looking at all p features,

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:56.250
the tree only considers a random subset of m features.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.200
A common default is m equals the square root of

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:05.170
p for classification, and p over three for regression.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.120
Because each split sees different features, the trees become less

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:14.470
correlated, so rho goes down and the averaged variance goes

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:16.160
down as well.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:21.110
Notice that we do not prune the trees in a

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:22.420
random forest.

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:27.370
Each tree is grown deep, with low bias, and the

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.200
averaging takes care of the variance.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:35.150
Now, one more nice thing about bagging is the out-of-bag

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.080
error.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:41.030
Remember that each bootstrap sample leaves out about 36.8 percent

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.720
of the data.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:47.670
For every training point, we can collect the trees that

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:52.020
did not see it, and use only those trees to

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:53.330
predict it.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.280
Averaging this error over all points gives the out-of-bag error,

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:01.870
which is almost like cross-validation, but for free.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:06.820
So you do not need a separate validation set to

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.270
tune the number of trees.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:14.220
A common mistake in the assignment last year was to

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:18.570
report the training accuracy of the forest, which is almost

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.540
one hundred percent, and call it the test accuracy.

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:25.590
Please do not do that.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.160
Let me show you a quick example with scikit-learn.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:35.110
We create RandomForestClassifier with n_estimators equal to five hundred and

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.180
oob_score equal to true.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:42.130
After fitting, the attribute oob_score_ gives you the out-of-bag accuracy

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:43.060
directly.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:48.010
Okay, for the midterm quiz next week, bagging, the 63.2

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.360
percent result, and the variance formula with rho are all

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:53.670
in scope.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:58.620
You do not need to memorise the proof, but you

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:02.970
should be able to explain why decorrelating the trees reduces

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.280
the variance.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:09.230
Alright, let's take a ten minute break, and after the

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.440
break we will move on to boosting.
